WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.060
A beta cell reads glucose by burning it, then releases insulin.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.260
Insulin must then act on liver, muscle and fat.

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.160
So when glucose climbs too high after a glucose drink, what failed?

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.660
Too little insulin, or insulin that works too weakly?

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.020
The usual test is that drink: 75 g of glucose, then blood glucose readings.

6
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:34.820
In a healthy person, glucose peaks near 140 to 160 mg/dL.

7
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.180
The curve blends three things: absorption, insulin secretion, and insulin action.

8
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:47.153
Reduce secretion, and muscle and fat take up less while the liver releases more.

9
00:00:48.593 --> 00:00:49.593
Glucose climbs higher.

10
00:00:50.853 --> 00:00:54.213
Now hold insulin constant and weaken its action instead.

11
00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:56.793
The same directions follow.

12
00:00:58.213 --> 00:01:03.113
Type 2 diabetes combines too little secretion with frequently reduced action.

13
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.013
Both faults tip one balance.

14
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:12.233
Blood glucose holds steady only while glucose entering the blood equals glucose

15
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:17.193
leaving it. Here, entry runs ahead of exit, so the level climbs.

16
00:01:18.553 --> 00:01:21.713
A curve shows that tipping, but not which fault caused it.

17
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:24.743
So hold glucose still on purpose.

18
00:01:26.103 --> 00:01:31.623
A hyperglycemic clamp holds glucose high and watches the insulin response: the beta

19
00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:35.543
cell's side. A second clamp asks about action.

20
00:01:36.703 --> 00:01:38.863
It holds insulin at a raised plateau.

21
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:45.403
A pump adds glucose, adjusted by repeated measurements, to keep glucose near basal.

22
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:50.763
Glucose is not changing, so glucose in equals glucose out.

23
00:01:52.203 --> 00:01:57.223
Disposal, the glucose leaving into tissues, equals the pump rate plus glucose the

24
00:01:57.223 --> 00:02:01.423
body still makes. That inner source is mostly the liver.

25
00:02:02.643 --> 00:02:05.643
This holds when almost no glucose is lost in urine.

26
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:10.657
Two healthy adults, same raised insulin, same steady glucose.

27
00:02:12.397 --> 00:02:13.677
Suppose the liver adds nothing.

28
00:02:15.177 --> 00:02:19.317
A's pump runs at 5.71 mg per kg per min.

29
00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:25.657
B's runs at 2.86. Whose tissues answer insulin more weakly?

30
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.750
B's. With nothing else entering, the pump rate is disposal.

31
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.750
B removes about half as much glucose at the same insulin.

32
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:48.390
That shows lower insulin-stimulated disposal, but not which step inside the cells is

33
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.390
weaker.

34
00:02:49.783 --> 00:02:53.423
But insulin cuts the liver's output and may not stop it.

35
00:02:54.463 --> 00:02:59.223
So that output is estimated with a tracer, distinguishably labeled glucose.

36
00:03:00.603 --> 00:03:04.883
Then disposal is the pump plus the liver's share, measured, not assumed.

37
00:03:05.983 --> 00:03:09.383
So a glucose curve shows the balance tipping, not why.

38
00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:13.563
Freeze the level, and the balance turns into a measurement.

39
00:03:14.863 --> 00:03:18.523
What the pump adds, plus what the body makes, is what the tissues take.
